{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8daa4bdd48789751","type":"tool_result","content":"===== RHAISTRAT-9006-E001.md =====\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9006-E001\ntitle: Select the Feast deployment topology for productization\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9006\ncomponent: Feature Store\nteam: RHOAI Feature Store\ntype: Investigation\npriority: P0\ndependencies: []\ninvestigation_signals:\n  question_specificity: 1\n  source_accessibility: 1\n  local_runnability: 1\n  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n  human_judgment_required: -1\nimplementation_type: null\nai_signals: null\nai_implementability: Medium\nai_implementability_score: 1\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Select the Feast deployment topology for productization\n\n## Description\n\nResolve which Feast deployment topology RHOAI productizes first — the operator-based\ndeployment or the standalone server per project — and produce a decision record that\nall downstream Feature Store, Platform Operator, and Dashboard work builds on. Upstream\nFeast supports both; supportability differs, and the choice determines what gets\npackaged, how administrators enable the component, and where per-project endpoints\ncome from.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Evaluate the operator-based deployment topology: deployment complexity, upgrade\n  path, multi-project behavior, resource footprint, security posture, support surface.\n- Evaluate the standalone-server-per-project topology against the same criteria.\n- Validate both topologies in a test environment (a live OpenShift cluster).\n- Document the decision: selected topology, rationale, rejected alternative, and\n  consequences for Platform Operator enablement, per-project endpoint exposure, and\n  Dashboard integration.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Both topologies evaluated against a defined, written set of supportability criteria\n  (deployment complexity, upgrade path, per-project isolation, failure modes,\n  support surface).\n- Both topologies exercised in a test environment; observations recorded.\n- Decision record written naming the selected topology with rationale and the\n  consequences for enablement, per-project endpoints, and Dashboard integration.\n- Decision reviewed and agreed with stakeholders.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (gating): determines how the Feature Store is deployed and enabled as a\n  supported component; every topology-dependent epic is blocked on this decision.\n\n===== RHAISTRAT-9006-E002.md =====\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9006-E002\ntitle: Onboard the Feast midstream repository under opendatahub-io\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9006\ncomponent: Feature Store\nteam: RHOAI Feature Store\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P0\nimplementation_type: repo-onboarding\ndependencies: []\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 1\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 0\n  existing_foundation: 1\n  open_questions: 1\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: -1\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 0\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: High\nai_implementability_score: 4\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Onboard the Feast midstream repository under opendatahub-io\n\n## Description\n\nCreate the productized midstream repository for Feast under the opendatahub-io\norganization so the platform can carry patches, build productized images, and run\nrelease automation for the Feature Store component. Today Feast exists only upstream,\nnext to the platform, with no productized repository.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Fork/mirror upstream Feast into a midstream repository under opendatahub-io,\n  following the conventions used by other RHOAI component midstream repositories.\n- Set up branch strategy, OWNERS/maintainers, and CI trigger configuration.\n- Document the upstream sync process.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Midstream Feast repository exists under opendatahub-io.\n- Branch and release conventions match other RHOAI midstream repositories.\n- Upstream sync process documented and executable.\n- Repository accessible to the owning team and build automation.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1: productized repository foundation for the supported Feature Store component\n  (no productized repository exists today).\n\n===== RHAISTRAT-9006-E003.md =====\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9006-E003\ntitle: Validate licenses for Feast and the Feast SDK dependency tree\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9006\ncomponent: Feature Store\nteam: RHOAI Feature Store\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P0\nimplementation_type: license-validation\ndependencies: []\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 1\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 0\n  existing_foundation: 1\n  open_questions: 1\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: 0\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 0\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: High\nai_implementability_score: 5\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Validate licenses for Feast and the Feast SDK dependency tree\n\n## Description\n\nRun license scanning over the Feast codebase and the Feast Python SDK — including\ntransitive dependencies — to confirm both are usable in supported RHOAI productized\nimages and workbench images, and to surface any restrictions that must be documented\nas supported limits.\n\n## Scope\n\n- License scan of the midstream Feast codebase (server, operator where applicable).\n- License scan of the Feast Python SDK and its transitive dependencies as they would\n  be installed in a supported workbench image.\n- Review flagged licenses; record resolutions (acceptable, exclusion, remediation).\n- Produce a go/no-go verdict and feed any restrictions into the documentation of\n  supported limits.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- License scans completed for the Feast codebase and the Feast SDK transitive\n  dependency tree.\n- Every flagged license reviewed with a documented resolution.\n- Go/no-go verdict recorded; any usage restrictions handed to the documentation\n  effort as supported-limits input.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1: licensing gate for shipping productized Feature Store images.\n- HLR-2: licensing gate for preinstalling the Feast SDK in a supported workbench\n  image.\n\n===== RHAISTRAT-9006-E004.md =====\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9006-E004\ntitle: Onboard the Feast repository to the Konflux build pipeline\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9006\ncomponent: Feature Store\nteam: RHOAI Feature Store\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P0\nimplementation_type: konflux-onboarding\ndependencies:\n- RHAISTRAT-9006-E002\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 1\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 1\n  existing_foundation: 1\n  open_questions: 1\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: 0\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 0\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: High\nai_implementability_score: 6\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Onboard the Feast repository to the Konflux build pipeline\n\n## Description\n\nConfigure the midstream Feast repository for Konflux so that productized Feature Store\ncontainer images build through the standard platform build and release pipeline,\nlike other RHOAI components.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Apply the known Konflux onboarding recipe to the midstream Feast repository.\n- Configure build recipes for the Feast container images the component ships.\n- Integrate with platform release automation consistent with other RHOAI components.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Konflux build pipeline configured for the midstream Feast repository.\n- At least one image build completes green through the pipeline.\n- Release automation integration matches other RHOAI components.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1: productized images for the supported Feature Store component require a\n  platform build pipeline (none exists today).\n\n===== RHAISTRAT-9006-E005.md =====\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9006-E005\ntitle: Preinstall the Feast SDK in a supported workbench image\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9006\ncomponent: Notebooks\nteam: RHOAI Notebooks\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P1\ndependencies:\n- RHAISTRAT-9006-E003\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 1\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 0\n  existing_foundation: 1\n  open_questions: 1\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: 0\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 0\nimplementation_type: null\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: High\nai_implementability_score: 5\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Preinstall the Feast SDK in a supported workbench image\n\n## Description\n\nAdd the Feast Python SDK to at least one supported RHOAI workbench image so data\nscientists can reach the feature store directly from their workbenches without\nmanual installation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Add the Feast SDK (license-cleared, pinned version) to a supported workbench image\n  definition.\n- Verify the SDK imports and basic client operations work against a reachable Feast\n  endpoint from the workbench.\n- Update workbench image metadata as needed.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Feast SDK preinstalled in at least one supported workbench image.\n- SDK imports successfully in the workbench; basic client operations verified against\n  a reachable Feature Store endpoint.\n- SDK version pinned and compatible with the productized Feature Store version.\n- Workbench image build green through its existing pipeline.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-2: data scientists can reach the feature store from workbenches with the Feast\n  SDK preinstalled in at least one supported workbench image.\n\n===== RHAISTRAT-9006-E006.md =====\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9006-E006\ntitle: Surface the Feature Store in the Dashboard\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9006\ncomponent: Dashboard\nteam: RHOAI Dashboard\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P1\ndependencies:\n- RHAISTRAT-9006-E001\ngated_by: RHAISTRAT-9006-E001\ngate_failure_impact:\n  action: rewrite\n  fallback_approach: Adapt the component-state source and per-project endpoint discovery\n    to whichever Feast deployment topology is selected; the core enabled-components\n    display and link-out work is unchanged.\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 1\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 1\n  existing_foundation: 1\n  open_questions: -1\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: 0\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 1\nimplementation_type: null\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: High\nai_implementability_score: 5\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\n---\n# Surface the Feature Store in the Dashboard\n\n## Description\n\nShow the Feature Store as an enabled component in the Dashboard and link users to its\nendpoints per project, following the existing enabled-component integration pattern\n(feature flag via the Dashboard configuration CR, navigation/landing presence,\nlink-out to the component).\n\n## Scope\n\n- Add the Feature Store to the Dashboard's enabled-components display.\n- Add per-project links to Feature Store endpoints.\n- Feature-flag the integration via the existing Dashboard configuration CR so it is\n  inactive unless the component is enabled.\n- Link-out only — no feature-authoring UI this release (explicitly out of scope in\n  the strategy).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Feature Store shown as an enabled component in projects where it is enabled.\n- Users can reach Feature Store endpoints via per-project links.\n- Integration feature-flagged; inactive when the component is not enabled.\n- Component-state source and endpoint discovery match the deployment topology\n  selected by the Feast deployment-topology investigation.\n- Verified against a Feature Store enabled in a test project.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-3: the Dashboard shows the Feature Store as an enabled component and links\n  users to its endpoints per project.\n\n===== RHAISTRAT-9006-E007.md =====\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9006-E007\ntitle: Document Feature Store enablement, getting started, and supported limits\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9006\ncomponent: Documentation\nteam: RHOAI Documentation\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P1\nimplementation_type: docs-authoring\ndependencies:\n- RHAISTRAT-9006-E002\n- RHAISTRAT-9006-E003\n- RHAISTRAT-9006-E004\n- RHAISTRAT-9006-E005\n- RHAISTRAT-9006-E006\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 1\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 0\n  existing_foundation: 1\n  open_questions: 0\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: -1\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 0\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: High\nai_implementability_score: 3\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Document Feature Store enablement, getting started, and supported limits\n\n## Description\n\nAuthor the product documentation for the Feature Store component: administrator\nenablement, a data-scientist getting-started walkthrough, and supported limits.\n\n## Scope\n\n- New documentation topic covering administrator enablement of the Feature Store\n  through the Platform Operator.\n- Getting-started walkthrough for data scientists starting from a workbench with the\n  preinstalled Feast SDK.\n- Supported-limits topic, including customer-managed offline-store storage (managed\n  offline-store infrastructure is out of scope) and topology-specific constraints\n  from the selected deployment topology.\n- Incidental updates to component overviews and enablement references that fall out\n  of the new topics.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Enablement documentation covers the administrator workflow end to end.\n- Getting-started walkthrough validated against the shipped workbench SDK and a\n  deployed Feature Store.\n- Supported limits documented, including bring-your-own offline store.\n- Technically reviewed against the implementation by